<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Gregory I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 540–604</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Gregory the Great, Saint Gregory the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope Gregory I, known as Gregory the Great, was pope from 590 to 604. He organised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s food supply and defence against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, making the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> the real power in the Duchy of Rome. In 596 he sent Augustine to convert the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxons</a>, a mission linked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> to his famous pun on Deiran slave boys.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i.jpg" alt="Pope Gregory I" width="320" height="349" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: British Library · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gregory_the_Great_with_the_Holy_Spirit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…he arranged for books ‘most necessary for all men to know’ to be put into English, among them <b>Gregory the Great</b>’s Pastoral Care, with a preface in his own name; English versions of Boethius’s Consolation of Philosophy, of Orosius’s history and of Bede’s Ecclesiastical History were also made…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>From the time of <b>Gregory the Great</b> (590–604) the bishops of Rome administered the extensive estates of the Roman church, fed the city and negotiated with the Lombards, and the Byzantine Duchy of Rome increasingly looked to the pope rather than to distant Constantinople.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Prose came later, inspired chiefly by Alfred the Great, who in the later 9th century advocated education in English alongside Latin and had many works translated, some, such as <b>Pope Gregory I</b>’s Pastoral Care, apparently by himself.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-english">Old English · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>It is attributed either to the Franciscan Thomas of Celano (1200–1265) or to Latino Malabranca Orsini (d. 1294), lector at the Dominican house of studies at Santa Sabina in Rome; some sources take it back to <b>Gregory the Great</b>, Bernard of Clairvaux or Bonaventure.</blockquote><a href="/songs/dies-irae">Dies irae</a></li><li><blockquote>As early as the 6th century Pope <b>Gregory the Great</b> noted that East and West sang it differently: in the Eastern churches everyone present sang it together, while in the West the clergy sang and the people responded, and the West sang &quot;Christe eleison&quot; (&quot;Christ, have mercy&quot;) as…</blockquote><a href="/songs/kyrie">Kyrie</a></li><li><blockquote>Sabinian was consecrated bishop of Rome, succeeding <b>Pope Gregory I</b>, who had died in March.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-13">13 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=581" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pope Gregory I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=581">What was here in 581 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…it is impossible to cut off every thing at once from their rude natures; because he who endeavours to ascend to the highest place rises by degrees or steps, and not by leaps.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Abbot Mellitus, 601 (Bede, Ecclesiastical History, book 1, chapter 30) &#183; tr. A. M. Sellar, 1907 &#183; his advice on the temples of the English, preserved by Bede &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/38326/pg38326-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For things are not to be loved for the sake of places, but places for the sake of good things.&#8221;</p><footer>Answers to Augustine of Canterbury (Bede, Ecclesiastical History, book 1, chapter 27) &#183; tr. A. M. Sellar, 1907 &#183; his answer to Augustine of Canterbury on the customs of the Churches, preserved by Bede &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/38326/pg38326-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…for they have an angelic face, and it is meet that such should be co-heirs with the Angels in heaven.&#8221;</p><footer>Bede, Ecclesiastical History, book 2, chapter 1 &#183; tr. A. M. Sellar, 1907 &#183; Bede's account of his words on seeing English boys for sale in Rome &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/38326/pg38326-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Choose, therefore, from every Church those things that are pious, religious, and right, and when you have, as it were, made them up into one bundle, let the minds of the English be accustomed thereto.&#8221;</p><footer>Answers to Augustine of Canterbury (Bede, Ecclesiastical History, book 1, chapter 27) &#183; tr. A. M. Sellar, 1907 &#183; on which customs the new English Church should keep, preserved by Bede &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/38326/pg38326-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
